div.p:
Ambassador Meeker, when he delivered to Ceausescu the Presidentʼs letter about the Summit, had to listen (cable at Tab A) to the Romanian Presidentʼs “doubts” about the Moscow communiqué and US-Soviet basic principles. Ceausescuʼs comments included:
—the SALT agreements did not take account of third state interests and offered no Soviet or US commitment not to use nuclear weapons;
—a resultant danger was that some third countries, for example India, would seek to acquire nuclear weapons and other cheap mass destruction weapons, such as lasers;
—the Moscow communiquéʼs language about US and Soviet respect for each otherʼs interests implies a joint purpose to establish an “equilibrium” between the two powers. But such an equilibrium would be “fragile” and could be upset by third countries.
